        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Write <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2 + \\dfrac{2}{k}]</span> as a single fraction.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Write [MATH: 2 + \\dfrac{2}{k}] as a single fraction.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Using the fact that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2=\\dfrac 2 1,]</span> we can write the expression as</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac 2 1 + \\dfrac{2}{k}.]</span> </p><p>In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: k]</span> by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: k\\mathbin{:}]</span></p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (k)/(k) \u22c5 (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)]</span></p><p>Finally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k + 2)/(k)|=; (2(k + 1))/(k)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Using the fact that [MATH: 2=\\dfrac 2 1,] we can write the expression as\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac 2 1 + \\dfrac{2}{k}.]\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: k] by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by [MATH: k\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (k)/(k) \u22c5 (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)]\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n[MATH: (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k + 2)/(k)|=; (2(k + 1))/(k)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: 2 + \\dfrac{2}{k}] as a single fraction.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the fact that [MATH: 2=\\dfrac 2 1,] we can write the expression as\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac 2 1 + \\dfrac{2}{k}.]\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: k] by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by [MATH: k\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (k)/(k) \u22c5 (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)]\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n[MATH: (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k + 2)/(k)|=; (2(k + 1))/(k)]"
      },
