        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Write <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p+1}]</span> as a single fraction.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Write [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p+1}] as a single fraction.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (p+1)q,]</span> since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. </p><p>To do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: p+1,]</span> and the top and bottom of the second fraction by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: q\\mathbin{:}]</span> </p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (1)/(q) + (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/(p + 1) \u22c5 (1)/(q) + (q)/(q) \u22c5 (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/((p + 1)q) + (q)/((p + 1)q)|=; (p + q + 1)/((p + 1)q)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: (p+1)q,] since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by [MATH: p+1,] and the top and bottom of the second fraction by [MATH: q\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (1)/(q) + (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/(p + 1) \u22c5 (1)/(q) + (q)/(q) \u22c5 (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/((p + 1)q) + (q)/((p + 1)q)|=; (p + q + 1)/((p + 1)q)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p+1}] as a single fraction.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIn this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: (p+1)q,] since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by [MATH: p+1,] and the top and bottom of the second fraction by [MATH: q\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (1)/(q) + (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/(p + 1) \u22c5 (1)/(q) + (q)/(q) \u22c5 (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/((p + 1)q) + (q)/((p + 1)q)|=; (p + q + 1)/((p + 1)q)]"
      },
