    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nRemember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like [MATH: \\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8,] we need to put the fractions over a common denominator, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (1)/(4) + (1)/(8)|=; (2)/(8) + (1)/(8)|=; (3)/(8)]\n\nContent:\nIt works the same in algebra! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression\n\nExample Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: 2 + \\dfrac{2}{k}] as a single fraction.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the fact that [MATH: 2=\\dfrac 2 1,] we can write the expression as\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac 2 1 + \\dfrac{2}{k}.]\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: k] by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by [MATH: k\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (k)/(k) \u22c5 (2)/(1) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)]\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n[MATH: (2k)/(k) + (2)/(k)|=; (2k + 2)/(k)|=; (2(k + 1))/(k)]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nWrite [MATH: 4 + (4)/(n)] as a single fraction.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (4n + 1)/(4n)]\nb. [MATH: (n + 4)/(n)]\nc. [MATH: (2(n + 1))/(n)]\nd. [MATH: (4(n + 1))/(n)]\ne. [MATH: (n + 4)/(4n)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nWrite [MATH: 3 \u2212 (a + 2)/(a \u2212 3)] as a single fraction.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (4a \u2212 7)/(a \u2212 3)]\nb. [MATH: (4a \u2212 11)/(a \u2212 3)]\nc. [MATH: (4a + 11)/(a \u2212 3)]\nd. [MATH: (2a \u2212 11)/(a \u2212 3)]\ne. [MATH: (2a \u2212 7)/(a \u2212 3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators\n\nExample Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p+1}] as a single fraction.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIn this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: (p+1)q,] since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by [MATH: p+1,] and the top and bottom of the second fraction by [MATH: q\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (1)/(q) + (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/(p + 1) \u22c5 (1)/(q) + (q)/(q) \u22c5 (1)/(p + 1)|=; (p + 1)/((p + 1)q) + (q)/((p + 1)q)|=; (p + q + 1)/((p + 1)q)]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (2x)/(y(x + 2)) \u2212 (3)/(2y) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (y + 2)/(2y(x + 2))]\nb. [MATH: (x \u2212 3)/(2(x + 2))]\nc. [MATH: (2x \u2212 3)/(2y(x + 2))]\nd. [MATH: (x \u2212 6)/(2y(x + 2))]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (6)/(2y(x + 2))]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (3x)/(x + 7) + (4y)/(y \u2212 8) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (7xy \u2212 24x + 28y)/((x + 7)(y \u2212 8))]\nb. [MATH: (\u2212 xy \u2212 2x \u2212 4y)/((x + 7)(y \u2212 8))]\nc. [MATH: (xy \u2212 x + y)/((x + 7)(y \u2212 8))]\nd. [MATH: (7xy + 4y)/((x + 7)(y \u2212 8))]\ne. [MATH: (7xy \u2212 9)/((x + 7)(y \u2212 8))]"
